#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059382 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059823 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fb626c84-db31-5e1b-90cb-3f5d13ee0af5" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Confidence Intervals From Large Samples"}] :question/id #uuid "97c8f1dc-747e-5aa4-ab4f-eb809abb560e" :question/math-academy-id "e-10035" :question/problem "Consider a sample of size $n=196$  from a population with standard deviation $\\sigma=7.$ Given that the mean of the sample is $\\overline{x}=24,$ find a $95\\%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu$ of the distribution.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(Z > 1.96) = 0.025,$ where $Z \\sim N(0,1),$ as shown in the diagram below.*\n\n![](images/28/28f56656e191e342a4815ba74bdd29cba5247fba7d8ab0feca8ffbe0b785ab8c.png)" :question/worked-solution "We do not know the distribution of the population. However, the sample size $n = 196 \\geq 30$ is *sufficiently large*. Therefore, according to the central limit theorem, we may use the following approximation:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\overline{X} - \\mu}{\\sigma/\\sqrt n} \\sim N(0,1),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $\\overline{X}$ is the sample mean,\n\n- $\\mu$ is the population mean,\n\n- $\\sigma^2$ is the population variance, and\n\n- $n$ is the sample size.\n\nGiven a value $\\alpha$ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha)]\\%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu$ of the original distribution is given by\n\n$$\n\\overline{x} \\pm z_{\\alpha/2} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\sigma}{\\sqrt{n}},\n$$\n\nwhere $P(Z > z_{\\alpha/2}) = \\dfrac{\\alpha}{2}$ and $Z \\sim N(0,1).$\n\nWe are interested in finding a $95\\%$ confidence interval. So, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha=1-0.95=0.05 \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\n\\dfrac{\\alpha}{2}=0.025.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(Z > 1.96) = 0.025.$ As a result, \n\n$$\nz_{\\alpha/2} = z_{0.025} = 1.96.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $95\\%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu$ is the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}24\\pm 1.96\\cdot \\frac{7}{\\sqrt{196}} \\\\ 24\\pm 0.98\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059383 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059824 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "27d20d28-3fba-5830-8bff-76e9a1c24bec" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Confidence Intervals: Applications"}] :question/id #uuid "3189a42a-200e-5caa-bc76-6ac1f4ae424d" :question/math-academy-id "e-10039" :question/problem "The daily percentage return of a particular stock has a population standard deviation of $\\sigma = 5\\%.$ A sample of $90$ randomly selected trading days was examined, and it was found that the mean daily return of the stock over these days was $7.5\\%.$ Find a $90\\%$ confidence interval for the mean daily percentage return $\\mu$ of the entire population of trading days.\n\n*Hint: Given a random variable $Z \\sim N(0,1),$ the table below shows the $z$-scores $z_p$ such that $P(Z > z_p) = p$ for some particular values of $p.$*\n\n| $p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $z_p$ | $1.282$ | $1.645$ | $1.960$ | $2.326$ | $2.576$ |" :question/worked-solution "We do not know the distribution of the population. However, the sample size $n = 90\\geq 30$ is *sufficiently large*. Therefore, according to the central limit theorem, we may use the following approximation:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\overline{X} - \\mu}{\\sigma/\\sqrt n} \\sim N(0,1),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $\\overline{X}$ is the sample mean,\n\n- $\\mu$ is the population mean,\n\n- $\\sigma^2$ is the population variance, and\n\n- $n$ is the sample size.\n\nGiven a value $\\alpha$ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha)]\\%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu$ of the original distribution is given by\n\n$$\n\\overline{x} \\pm z_{\\alpha/2} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\sigma}{\\sqrt{n}},\n$$\n\nwhere $P(Z > z_{\\alpha/2}) = \\dfrac{\\alpha}{2}$ and $Z \\sim N(0,1).$\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\n\\overline{x}=7.5, \\qquad\nn = 90, \\qquad\n\\sigma = 5.\n$$\n\nWe are interested in finding a $90\\%$ confidence interval. So, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha=1-0.9=0.1 \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\n\\dfrac{\\alpha}{2}=0.05.\n$$\n\nWe also need to find the $z$-score value $z_{0.05}$ such that $P(Z > z_{0.05}) = 0.05,$ where $Z \\sim N(0,1).$\n\n![](images/2f/2fc6327df99c6b88508b0c4de2e4edf6c5521974f05e2e2f02d09786937285a7.png)\n\nFrom the percentage points table of the normal distribution, we obtain that $z_{0.05}=1.645{:}$ \n\n| $p$ | $0.100$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}0.050}$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $z_p$ | $1.282$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}1.645}$ | $1.960$ | $2.326$ | $2.576$ |\n\nTherefore, the $90\\%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu$ is the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7.5\\pm 1.645\\cdot \\frac{5}{\\sqrt{90}} \\\\ 7.5\\pm 0.867\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059384 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059825 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b90997d0-b563-5541-9fe2-36d7457da7b5" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Confidence Intervals From Normal Populations"}] :question/id #uuid "3a166c80-cc70-5dd3-9dbe-2798dc882199" :question/math-academy-id "e-10046" :question/problem "Consider a sample of size $n=9$ from a normal population with standard deviation $\\sigma=2.$ Given that the mean of the sample is $\\overline{x}=-5,$ find a $90\\%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu$ of the distribution.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(Z > 1.645) = 0.05,$ where $Z \\sim N(0,1),$ as shown in the diagram below.*\n\n![](images/39/39a83b1081d22919c9549c4ff1c6ad8006dc802cc23f9802d762e97883bea48c.png)" :question/worked-solution "We are told that the population is normally distributed. Therefore, the distribution of sample mean is also normal, and we have that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\overline{X} - \\mu}{\\sigma/\\sqrt n} \\sim N(0,1),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $\\overline{X}$ is the sample mean,\n\n- $\\mu$ is the population mean,\n\n- $\\sigma^2$ is the population variance, and\n\n- $n$ is the sample size.\n\nGiven a value $\\alpha$ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha)]\\%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu$ of the original distribution is given by\n\n$$\n\\overline{x} \\pm z_{\\alpha/2} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\sigma}{\\sqrt{n}},\n$$\n\nwhere $P(Z > z_{\\alpha/2}) = \\dfrac{\\alpha}{2}$ and $Z \\sim N(0,1).$\n\nWe are interested in finding a $90\\%$ confidence interval. So, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha=1-0.9=0.1 \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\n\\dfrac{\\alpha}{2}=0.05.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(Z > 1.645) = 0.05.$ As a result, \n\n$$\nz_{\\alpha/2} = z_{0.05} = 1.645.\n$$\n\nTherefore, a $90\\%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu$ is the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-5\\pm 1.645\\cdot \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{9}} \\\\ -5\\pm 1.097\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060051 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059824 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "27d20d28-3fba-5830-8bff-76e9a1c24bec" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Confidence Intervals: Applications"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2edd902d-c81c-5e22-aba3-b2fec1772d18" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pm 3.136\\,\\text{oz.}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498837} {:answer/id #uuid "34b45810-bb9c-507b-9239-9a751130bc6a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8\\pm 3.136\\,\\text{oz.}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498849} {:answer/id #uuid "6b4eade3-b8df-5f9b-be5b-4876c06523fa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8\\pm 2.576\\,\\text{oz.}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498972} {:answer/id #uuid "7e6c9006-9b4d-5e16-96e1-d67231ba5341" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8\\pm 0.392\\,\\text{oz.}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499015} {:answer/id #uuid "e8160460-55bd-51bf-99c6-01f01dfbfe41" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pm 0.576\\,\\text{oz.}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499256}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7e6c9006-9b4d-5e16-96e1-d67231ba5341" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8\\pm 0.392\\,\\text{oz.}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499015} :answer-field/id #uuid "fc3ba565-a19b-5532-bf36-77f306225f27" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186056664}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "e3e94e18-ed0a-5b05-b424-382dd894f561" :question/math-academy-id "q-142334" :question/problem "The weight of potatoes grown in a particular state is distributed with a population standard deviation of $\\sigma =2\\,\\text{oz}.$ A farmer sampled $100$ potatoes from his field and found that their mean weight is $8\\,\\text{oz}.$ Find a $95%$ confidence interval for the population mean weight $\\mu $ for the entire state. \n\n*Hint: Given a random variable $Z∼N(0,1),$ the table below shows the $z$-scores ${z}_{p}$ such that $P(Z>{z}_{p})=p$ for some particular values of $p.$*\n\n| $p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${z}_{p}$ | $1.282$ | $1.645$ | $1.960$ | $2.326$ | $2.576$ |" :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "We do not know the distribution of the population. However, the sample size $n=100\\ge 30$ is *sufficiently large*. Therefore, according to the central limit theorem, we may use the following approximation:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\overset{―}{X}-\\mu }{\\sigma /\\sqrt{n}}∼N(0,1),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $\\overset{―}{X}$ is the sample mean,\n\n- $\\mu $ is the population mean,\n\n- ${\\sigma }^{2}$ is the population variance, and\n\n- $n$ is the sample size.\n\nGiven a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ of the original distribution is given by\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}\\pm {z}_{\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\frac{\\sigma }{\\sqrt{n}},\n$$\n\nwhere $P(Z>{z}_{\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2}$ and $Z∼N(0,1).$\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}=8\\,\\text{oz},\\,n=100,\\,\\sigma =2\\,\\text{oz}.\n$$\n\nWe are interested in finding a $95%$ confidence interval. So, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.95=0.05\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.025.\n$$\n\nWe also need to find the $z$-score value ${z}_{0.025}$ such that $P(Z>{z}_{0.025})=0.025,$ where $Z∼N(0,1).$\n\n![](images/35/35037db1532092651e1974ea689b6004aa673c29720ac1d1c9d191c5929e9314.png)\n\nFrom the percentage points table of the normal distribution, we obtain that ${z}_{0.025}=1.96:$ \n\n| $p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${z}_{p}$ | $1.282$ | $1.645$ | $1.960$ | $2.326$ | $2.576$ |\n\nTherefore, the $95%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ is the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8\\pm 1.96\\cdot \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{100}} \\\\ 8\\pm 0.392\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186499458 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059823 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fb626c84-db31-5e1b-90cb-3f5d13ee0af5" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Confidence Intervals From Large Samples"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3cf51c58-8076-5870-ab2b-eb1fc2539181" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8\\pm 2.632" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498870} {:answer/id #uuid "8ab56bd2-ce09-587d-843b-27bd1a6984d0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8\\pm 0.823" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499039} {:answer/id #uuid "ae860d4c-2368-596d-92f3-ad66058bfdb2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\pm 0.103" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499115} {:answer/id #uuid "f17522f7-78ab-5431-828f-7a837990e587" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8\\pm 0.103" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499272} {:answer/id #uuid "f66bb9d9-231b-5727-aa05-2e32febfa1d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\pm 0.823" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499280}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8ab56bd2-ce09-587d-843b-27bd1a6984d0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8\\pm 0.823" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499039} :answer-field/id #uuid "a81e5efe-662a-598e-a045-0d6af521e7e9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499391}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3aeeb390-5562-50bf-80f7-9c922c7da785" :question/math-academy-id "q-142495" :question/problem "![](images/39/39a83b1081d22919c9549c4ff1c6ad8006dc802cc23f9802d762e97883bea48c.png)\n\nConsider a sample of size $n=64$  from a population with standard deviation $\\sigma =4.$ Given that the mean of the sample is $\\overset{―}{x}=8,$ find a $90%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ of the distribution.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(Z>1.645)=0.05,$ where $Z∼N(0,1),$ as shown in the diagram above.*" :question/worked-solution "We do not know the distribution of the population. However, the sample size $n=64\\ge 30$ is *sufficiently large*. Therefore, according to the central limit theorem, we may use the following approximation:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\overset{―}{X}-\\mu }{\\sigma /\\sqrt{n}}∼N(0,1),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $\\overset{―}{X}$ is the sample mean,\n\n- $\\mu $ is the population mean,\n\n- ${\\sigma }^{2}$ is the population variance, and\n\n- $n$ is the sample size.\n\nGiven a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ of the original distribution is given by\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}\\pm {z}_{\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\frac{\\sigma }{\\sqrt{n}},\n$$\n\nwhere $P(Z>{z}_{\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2}$ and $Z∼N(0,1).$\n\nWe are interested in finding a $90%$ confidence interval. So, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.90=0.10\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.05.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(Z>1.645)=0.05.$ As a result, \n\n$$\n{z}_{\\alpha /2}={z}_{0.05}=1.645.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $90%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ is the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8\\pm 1.645\\cdot \\frac{4}{\\sqrt{64}} \\\\ 8\\pm 0.823\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186499553 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059823 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fb626c84-db31-5e1b-90cb-3f5d13ee0af5" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Confidence Intervals From Large Samples"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2f749dea-d684-5136-a3d2-84f63dff052e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-15\\pm 1.219" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498841} {:answer/id #uuid "7639e34c-b159-5e02-acf8-712862c1978d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-15\\pm 1.645" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498996} {:answer/id #uuid "d2d25afa-4f5b-56be-a5e8-9ac1007ee2ce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5.4\\pm 1.097" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499198} {:answer/id #uuid "d4e88cef-7eb2-51cb-ac6c-f873e1cd9f92" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5.4\\pm 1.219" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499206} {:answer/id #uuid "f29deb35-6d67-5eb2-9f14-2080837e4c17" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-15\\pm 1.097" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499276}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f29deb35-6d67-5eb2-9f14-2080837e4c17" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-15\\pm 1.097" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499276} :answer-field/id #uuid "98f862f6-df7c-5829-b5f0-70de3edf4727" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499388}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e735b6cb-9b8a-5d37-9b58-b848a68baaea" :question/math-academy-id "q-142496" :question/problem "![](images/39/39a83b1081d22919c9549c4ff1c6ad8006dc802cc23f9802d762e97883bea48c.png)\n\nConsider a sample of size $n=81$  from a population with standard deviation $\\sigma =6.$ Given that the mean of the sample is $\\overset{―}{x}=-15,$ find a $90%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ of the distribution.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(Z>1.645)=0.05,$ where $Z∼N(0,1),$ as shown in the diagram above.*" :question/worked-solution "We do not know the distribution of the population. However, the sample size $n=81\\ge 30$ is *sufficiently large*. Therefore, according to the central limit theorem, we may use the following approximation:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\overset{―}{X}-\\mu }{\\sigma /\\sqrt{n}}∼N(0,1),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $\\overset{―}{X}$ is the sample mean,\n\n- $\\mu $ is the population mean,\n\n- ${\\sigma }^{2}$ is the population variance, and\n\n- $n$ is the sample size.\n\nGiven a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ of the original distribution is given by\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}\\pm {z}_{\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\frac{\\sigma }{\\sqrt{n}},\n$$\n\nwhere $P(Z>{z}_{\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2}$ and $Z∼N(0,1).$\n\nWe are interested in finding a $90%$ confidence interval. So, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.90=0.10\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.05.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(Z>1.645)=0.05.$ As a result, \n\n$$\n{z}_{\\alpha /2}={z}_{0.05}=1.645.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $90%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ is the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-15\\pm 1.645\\cdot \\frac{6}{\\sqrt{81}} \\\\ -15\\pm 1.097\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186498542 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059823 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fb626c84-db31-5e1b-90cb-3f5d13ee0af5" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Confidence Intervals From Large Samples"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "26f43a97-858d-5d6b-8e14-2ca1af1707b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\pm 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497877} {:answer/id #uuid "419aefd4-69a2-580b-b986-0da3f154ef5a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "135\\pm 2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497947} {:answer/id #uuid "4d69db6f-7ccc-5702-a556-c06d016e9316" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\pm 1.645" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497980} {:answer/id #uuid "4e998fe4-61fb-520e-8733-db8847569f9f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "135\\pm 3.29" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497986} {:answer/id #uuid "74c43959-d0c0-50b0-94f0-843272fe1211" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\pm 0.142" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498078}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "74c43959-d0c0-50b0-94f0-843272fe1211" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\pm 0.142" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498078} :answer-field/id #uuid "77f21f06-2475-57bd-a2cb-3044a83d5e18" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498417}] :question/difficulty {:db/