\\mu }{\\sigma /\\sqrt{n}}∼N(0,1),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $\\overset{―}{X}$ is the sample mean,\n\n- $\\mu $ is the population mean,\n\n- ${\\sigma }^{2}$ is the population variance, and\n\n- $n$ is the sample size.\n\nGiven a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ of the original distribution is given by\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}\\pm {z}_{\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\frac{\\sigma }{\\sqrt{n}},\n$$\n\nwhere $P(Z>{z}_{\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2}$ and $Z∼N(0,1).$\n\nWe are interested in finding a $99%$ confidence interval. So, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.99=0.01\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.005.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(Z>2.576)=0.005.$ As a result, \n\n$$\n{z}_{\\alpha /2}={z}_{0.005}=2.576.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $99%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ is the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-12\\pm 2.576\\cdot \\frac{4}{\\sqrt{225}} \\\\ -12\\pm 0.687\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186498518 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059825 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b90997d0-b563-5541-9fe2-36d7457da7b5" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Confidence Intervals From Normal Populations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "01f96de5-dbdf-5045-bfec-c96bf5b0e350" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "22\\pm 0.823" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497802} {:answer/id #uuid "18db88ac-9193-5677-bce3-d86fd6469a6e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "19\\pm 1.156" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497857} {:answer/id #uuid "2bc03372-7346-5af4-aa8f-947357c87bc6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16\\pm 1.123" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497889} {:answer/id #uuid "355a5122-c31e-5d74-bdf3-1044eb577532" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "22\\