ha }{2}$ and $Z∼N(0,1).$\n\nWe are interested in finding a $90%$ confidence interval. So, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.9=0.1\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.05.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(Z>1.645)=0.05.$ As a result, \n\n$$\n{z}_{\\alpha /2}={z}_{0.05}=1.645.\n$$\n\nTherefore, a $90%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ is the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}22\\pm 1.645\\cdot \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{16}} \\\\ 22\\pm 0.823\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186499492 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059825 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b90997d0-b563-5541-9fe2-36d7457da7b5" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Confidence Intervals From Normal Populations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0bb590e3-9e88-5134-a516-751ae59cd403" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-280\\pm 13.450" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498749} {:answer/id #uuid "7af48d0b-43fe-5c01-8e01-de39581a6485" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "45\\pm 12.564" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499004} {:answer/id #uuid "8b2ff6a6-7a42-596a-a264-79e3ac0c52ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "43\\pm 13.450" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499040} {:answer/id #uuid "a57dc716-cf83-51b3-9230-d4bbe5f13613" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-280\\pm 28.361" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499089} {:answer/id #uuid "cf1b1742-cb11-50bc-93b0-32c17b0279e4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-280\\pm 12.564" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499187}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cf1b1742-cb11-50bc-93b0-32c17b0279e4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-280\\pm 12.564" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499187} :answer-field/id #uuid "53ad5430-a39e-5f1e-b7a8-63924d5fbc19" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499356}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "795def41-6dcd-54b5-9baf-91c8a014878e" :question/math-academy-id "q-142971" :question/problem "![](images/28/28f56656e191e342a4815ba74bdd29cba5247fba7d8ab0feca8ffbe0b785ab8c.png)\n\nConsider a sample of size $n=45$ from a normal population with standard deviation $\\sigma =43.$ Given that the mean of the sample is $\\overset{―}{x}=-280,$ find a $95%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ of the distribution.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(Z>1.96)=0.025,$ where $Z∼N(0,1),$ as shown in the diagram above.*" :question/worked-solution "We are told that the population is normally distributed. Therefore, the distribution of sample mean is also normal, and we have that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\overset{―}{X}-\\mu }{\\sigma /\\sqrt{n}}∼N(0,1),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $\\overset{―}{X}$ is the sample mean,\n\n- $\\mu $ is the population mean,\n\n- ${\\sigma }^{2}$ is the population variance, and\n\n- $n$ is the sample size.\n\nGiven a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ of the original distribution is given by\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}\\pm {z}_{\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\frac{\\sigma }{\\sqrt{n}},\n$$\n\nwhere $P(Z>{z}_{\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2}$ and $Z∼N(0,1).$\n\nWe are interested in finding a $95%$ confidence interval. So, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.95=0.05\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.025.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(Z>1.96)=0.025.$ As a result, \n\n$$\n{z}_{\\alpha /2}={z}_{0.025}=1.96.\n$$\n\nTherefore, a $95%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ is the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & -280\\pm 1.96\\cdot \\frac{43}{\\sqrt{45}} \\\\  & -280\\pm 12.564\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186498508 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059825 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b90997d0-b563-5541-9fe2-36d7457da7b5" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Confidence Intervals From Normal Populations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "58f93396-bb63-5e2d-9be2-5aff06d1100c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9\\pm 1.899" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498009} {:answer/id #uuid "668c3880-6cf7-5f86-8d9d-c48f288c2489" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9\\pm 2.193" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498043} {:answer/id #uuid "8299b9d2-aeff-5a18-a5df-fba1e994818a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12\\pm 1.899" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498103} {:answer/id #uuid "b0947152-9db1-51dc-96a4-f6cccad66ada" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9\\pm 0.731" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498192} {:answer/id #uuid "d3cba11e-5f53-5e85-aa22-88f50ef82d60" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12\\pm 2.193" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498275}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "58f93396-bb63-5e2d-9be2-5aff06d1100c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9\\pm 1.899" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498009} :answer-field/id #uuid "f02c5f87-b3cc-5c21-82fd-c00777a3a5e2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498479}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2b3720b9-719b-5e5b-8310-cbdf4c3bbfe3" :question/math-academy-id "q-142973" :question/problem "![](images/39/39a83b1081d22919c9549c4ff1c6ad8006dc802cc23f9802d762e97883bea48c.png)\n\nConsider a sample of size $n=12$ from a normal population with standard deviation $\\sigma =4.$ Given that the mean of the sample is $\\overset{―}{x}=9,$ find a $90%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ of the distribution.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(Z>1.645)=0.05,$ where $Z∼N(0,1),$ as shown in the diagram above.*" :question/worked-solution "We are told that the population is normally distributed. Therefore, the distribution of sample mean is also normal, and we have that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\overset{―}{X}-\\mu }{\\sigma /\\sqrt{n}}∼N(0,1),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $\\overset{―}{X}$ is the sample mean,\n\n- $\\mu $ is the population mean,\n\n- ${\\sigma }^{2}$ is the population variance, and\n\n- $n$ is the sample size.\n\nGiven a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ of the original distribution is given by\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}\\pm {z}_{\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\frac{\\sigma }{\\sqrt{n}},\n$$\n\nwhere $P(Z>{z}_{\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2}$ and $Z∼N(0,1).$\n\nWe are interested in finding a $90%$ confidence interval. So, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.9=0.1\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.05.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(Z>1.645)=0.05.$ As a result, \n\n$$\n{z}_{\\alpha /2}={z}_{0.05}=1.645.\n$$\n\nTherefore, a $90%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ is the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9\\pm 1.645\\cdot \\frac{4}{\\sqrt{12}} \\\\ 9\\pm 1.899\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186498501 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059825 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b90997d0-b563-5541-9fe2-36d7457da7b5" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Confidence Intervals From Normal Populations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "23f3c558-8e9a-5a12-bf1d-0fa1df352593" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-67\\pm 9.246" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497874} {:answer/id #uuid "510dac73-7b74-5411-b745-977232bca39f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 1759218604