the sample mean,\n\n- $\\mu $ is the population mean,\n\n- ${\\sigma }^{2}$ is the population variance, and\n\n- $n$ is the sample size.\n\nGiven a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ of the original distribution is given by\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}\\pm {z}_{\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\frac{\\sigma }{\\sqrt{n}},\n$$\n\nwhere $P(Z>{z}_{\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2}$ and $Z∼N(0,1).$\n\nWe are interested in finding a $90%$ confidence interval. So, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.9=0.1\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.05.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(Z>1.645)=0.05.$ As a result, \n\n$$\n{z}_{\\alpha /2}={z}_{0.05}=1.645.\n$$\n\nTherefore, a $90%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ is the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-67\\pm 1.645\\cdot \\frac{18}{\\sqrt{32}} \\\\ -67\\pm 5.234\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186499548 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059825 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b90997d0-b563-5541-9fe2-36d7457da7b5" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Confidence Intervals From Normal Populations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2f719a65-60e7-57dd-a27a-744f2bd0570e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "22.1\\pm 6.393" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498840} {:answer/id #uuid "710f42eb-828f-5104-9ba6-89eed5ec19de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9.4\\pm 6.918" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498983} {:answer/id #uuid "82a41cab-338c-5c2e-9f93-261e0ef79794" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12.7\\pm 9.430" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499024} {:answer/id #uuid "a62bc4bc-dfa0-54e7-b656-2ab71ae04f6c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9.4\\pm 4.930" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499092} {:answer/id #uuid "e187b3cc-ab28-5