onfidence interval. So, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.99=0.01\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.005.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(Z>2.576)=0.005.$ As a result, \n\n$$\n{z}_{\\alpha /2}={z}_{0.005}=2.576.\n$$\n\nTherefore, a $99%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ is the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & 12.7\\pm 2.576\\cdot \\frac{9.4}{\\sqrt{35}} \\\\  & 12.7\\pm 4.093\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186499552 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059824 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "27d20d28-3fba-5830-8bff-76e9a1c24bec" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Confidence Intervals: Applications"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "20648a1f-c753-53bc-9f17-f426238794a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.7\\pm 0.165\\,\\text{kg}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498803} {:answer/id #uuid "2a1fbae4-08c1-51c4-9103-d5db470cb219" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.3\\pm 0.318\\,\\text{kg}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498819} {:answer/id #uuid "643bccf8-7685-5615-8866-0adacabd70dc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.9\\pm 0.318\\,\\text{kg}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498954} {:answer/id #uuid "935e530b-6914-5f68-b340-194ff37f505b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.3\\pm 0.206\\,\\text{kg}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499050} {:answer/id #uuid "e82d23ba-fcc6-584a-9342-34a0789cc538" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.7\\pm 0.206\\,\\text{kg}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499257}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "935e530b-6914-5f68-b340-194ff37f505b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.3\\pm 0.206\\,\\text{kg}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499050} :answer-field/id #uuid "4c58b256-85c4-5e6e-847c-57f880f6c39c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499350}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e6c6accb-373d-5aa8-b43f-81ebbf038603" :question/math-academy-id "q-142989" :question/problem "The weight of metal sheets produced by a particular machine is normally distributed with standard deviation $\\sigma =0.4\\,\\text{kg}.$ During a quality check, $25$ metal sheets are sampled from the daily production, and it is found that their mean weight is $2.3\\,\\text{kg}.$ Find a $99%$ confidence interval for the population mean weight $\\mu $ for the entire production. \n\n*Hint: Given a random variable $Z∼N(0,1),$ the table below shows the $z$-scores ${z}_{p}$ such that $P(Z>{z}_{p})=p$ for some particular values of $p.$*\n\n| $p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${z}_{p}$ | $1.282$ | $1.645$ | $1.960$ | $2.326$ | $2.576$ |" :question/worked-solution "We are told that the population is normally distributed. Therefore, the distribution of sample mean is also normal, and we have that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\overset{―}{X}-\\mu }{\\sigma /\\sqrt{n}}∼N(0,1),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $\\overset{―}{X}$ is the sample mean,\n\n- $\\mu $ is the population mean,\n\n- ${\\sigma }^{2}$ is the population variance, and\n\n- $n$ is the sample size.\n\nGiven a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ of the original distribution is given by\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}\\pm {z}_{\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\frac{\\sigma }{\\sqrt{n}},\n$$\n\nwhere $P(Z>{z}_{\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2}$ and $Z∼N(0,1).$\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}=2.3\\,\\text{kg},\\,n=25,\\,\\sigma =0.4\\,\\text{kg}.\n$$\n\nWe are interested in finding a $99%$ confidence interval. So, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.99=0.01\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.005.\n$$\n\nWe also need to find the $z$-score value ${z}_{0.005}$ such that $P(Z>{z}_{0.005})=0.005,$ where $Z∼N(0,1).$\n\n![](images/8c/8c5ac93fdb7b03a2bf9cad38c9e5687867a2fa2968120586f3bf2e6101c24cbe.png)\n\nFrom the percentage points table of the normal distribution, we obtain that ${z}_{0.005}=2.576:$ \n\n| $p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${z}_{p}$ | $1.282$ | $1.645$ | $1.960$ | $2.326$ | $2.576$ |\n\nTherefore, the $99%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ is the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2.3\\pm 2.576\\cdot \\frac{0.4}{\\sqrt{25}} \\\\ 2.3\\pm 0.206\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186498557 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059824 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "27d20d28-3fba-5830-8bff-76e9a1c24bec" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Confidence Intervals: Applications"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "155ad1bd-d997-50f4-ab96-9007afb194f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.75\\pm 0.13\\,s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497848} {:answer/id #uuid "327a0039-b06e-53dd-bfc8-383328623244" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.55\\pm 0.03\\,s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497906} {:answer/id #uuid "8e3795ed-bdaf-5738-93ed-2a1185daa06c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.2\\pm 0.08\\,s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498126} {:answer/id #uuid "cac0a256-72d4-5ce5-8410-231dbe55c448" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.55\\pm 0.08\\,s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498251} {:answer/id #uuid "cbfcdb9f-6c30-5b43-b4de-181ea4940af1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.2\\pm 0.03\\,s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498258}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cac0a256-72d4-5ce5-8410-231dbe55c448" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.55\\pm 0.08\\,s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498251} :answer-field/id #uuid "74b47122-e0ef-5f16-b996-8efddca3bab2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498413}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "841adb19-e83e-5b6e-be60-db36b3558aba" :question/math-academy-id "q-143022" :question/problem "In order to measure the acceleration due to gravity, Annie knocks a ball off a table and records the time it takes to reach the ground. The measurements of the falling time of the ball are normally distributed with standard deviation $\\sigma =0.2\\,s.$ She repeats the experiment $16$ times and obtains a mean falling time of $0.55\\,s.$ Find a $90%$ confidence interval for the mean falling time $\\mu .$\n\n*Hint: Given a random variable $Z∼N(0,1),$ the table below shows the $z$-scores ${z}_{p}$ such that $P(Z>{z}_{p})=p$ for some particular values of $p.$*\n\n| $p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${z}_{p}$ | $1.282$ | $1.645$ | $1.960$ | $2.326$ | $2.576$ |" :question/worked-solution "We are told that the population is normally distributed. Therefore, the distribution of sample mean is also normal, and we have that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\overset{―}{X}-\\mu }{\\sigma /\\sqrt{n}}∼N(0,1),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $\\overset{―}{X}$ is the sample mean,\n\n- $\\mu $ is the population mean,\n\n- ${\\sigma }^{2}$ is the population variance, and\n\n- $n$ is the sample size.\n\nGiven a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ of the original distribution is given by\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}\\pm {z}_{\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\frac{\\sigma }{\\sqrt{n}},\n$$\n\nwhere $P(Z>{z}_{\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2}$ and $Z∼N(0,1).$\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}=0.55\\,s,\\,n=16,\\,\\sigma =0.2\\,s\n$$\n\nWe are interested in finding a $90%$ confidence interval. So, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.9=0.1\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.05.\n$$\n\nWe also need to find the $z$-score value ${z}_{0.05}$ such that $P(Z>{z}_{0.05})=0.05,$ where $Z∼N(0,1).$\n\n![](images/2f/2fc6327df99c6b88508b0c4de2e4edf6c5521974f05e2e2f02d09786937285a7.png)\n\nFrom the percentage points table of the normal distribution, we obtain that ${z}_{0.05}=1.645:$ \n\n| $p$ | $0.100$ | $0.05$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${z}_{p}$ | $1.282$ | $1.645$ | $1.960$ | $2.326$ | $2.576$ |\n\nTherefore, the $90%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ is the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0.55\\pm 1.645\\cdot \\frac{0.2}{\\sqrt{16}} \\\\ 0.55\\pm 0.08\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186499443 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059824 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "27d20d28-3fba-5830-8bff-76e9a1c24bec" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Confidence Intervals: Applications"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "48f20e6a-9608-55b0-904b-e2dd64d63ee1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$255\\pm 3.66$ words" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498897} {:answer/id #uuid "688c9ce6-4b4e-5a38-a9b2-06226b46abd3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$255\\pm 7.06$ words" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498968} {:answer/id #uuid "9f796349-2ad0-566d-a3dd-4c798b32bcd2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$36\\pm 3.66$ words" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499068} {:answer/id #uuid "de16784e-22c3-56ac-8038-f956c408ebf8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$255\\pm 5.26$ words" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499223} {:answer/id #uuid "f8383bc3-1ee0-58af-8254-11d85b748de9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$36\\pm 5.26$ words" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499286}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "de16784e-22c3-56ac-8038-f956c408ebf8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$255\\pm 5.26$ words" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499223} :answer-field/id #uuid "90532975-7885-57b5-8831-bf9748a043d8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499385}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "094a5111-7dbf-54d8-b6a9-b1a46e16163d" :question/math-academy-id "q-143023" :question/problem "A primary school teacher records how many words his second-year students can read in 5 minutes. The number of words read is distributed with a population standard deviation of $\\sigma =36$ words. In a school of $180$ students, he finds that the mean number of read words is $255$ words. Find a $95%$ confidence interval for the mean number of words $\\mu $ for the entire population of second-year children.\n\n*Hint: Given a random variable $Z∼N(0,1),$ the table below shows the $z$-scores ${z}_{p}$ such that $P(Z>{z}_{p})=p$ for some particular values of $p.$*\n\n| $p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${z}_{p}$ | $1.282$ | $1.645$ | $1.960$ | $2.326$ | $2.576$ |" :question/worked-solution "We do not know the distribution of the population. However, the sample size $n=180\\ge 30$ is *sufficiently large*. Therefore, according to the central limit theorem, we may use the following approximation:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\overset{―}{X}-\\mu }{\\sigma /\\sqrt{n}}∼N(0,1),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $\\overset{―}{X}$ is the sample mean,\n\n- $\\mu $ is the population mean,\n\n- ${\\sigma }^{2}$ is the population variance, and\n\n- $n$ is the sample size.\n\nGiven a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ of the original distribution is given by\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}\\pm {z}_{\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\frac{\\sigma }{\\sqrt{n}},\n$$\n\nwhere $P(Z>{z}_{\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2}$ and $Z∼N(0,1).$\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}=255,\\,n=180,\\,\\sigma =36.\n$$\n\nWe are interested in finding a $95%$ confidence interval. So, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.95=0.05\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.025.\n$$\n\nWe also need to find the $z$-score value ${z}_{0.025}$ such that $P(Z>{z}_{0.025})=0.025,$ where $Z∼N(0,1).$\n\n![](images/35/35037db1532092651e1974ea689b6004aa673c29720ac1d1c9d191c5929e9314.png)\n\nFrom the percentage points table of the normal distribution, we obtain that ${z}_{0.025}=1.96:$ \n\n| $p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${z}_{p}$ | $1.282$ | $1.645$ | $1.960$ | $2.326$ | $2.576$ |\n\nTherefore, the $95%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ is the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}255\\pm 1.96\\cdot \\frac{36}{\\sqrt{180}} \\\\ 255\\pm 5.26\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186498524 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059824 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "27d20d28-3fba-5830-8bff-76e9a1c24bec" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Confidence Intervals: Applications"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "44c8f4e3-9a6e-50ab-a851-2ca0685d61a8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "49.5\\pm 4.12\\,cm" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497953} {:answer/id #uuid "4593f573-da01-5e58-91b2-b20a7f84bb52" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "49.5\\pm 1.22\\,cm" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497955} {:answer/id #uuid "5b2069e1-e324-5627-8cfa-51a17d78e4d0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "49.5\\pm 3.14\\,cm" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498014} {:answer/id #uuid "75a879ce-e38a-59c6-9cde-2e3f1b82a578" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "49.5\\pm 1.61\\,cm" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498079} {:answer/id #uuid "fc5b7218-6c68-5e18-b2b9-35212faa3729" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "49.5\\pm 1.64\\,cm" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498348}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "75a879ce-e38a-59c6-9cde-2e3f1b82a578" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "49.5\\pm 1.61\\,cm" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498079} :answer-field/id #uuid "6bd35d8c-117b-596a-9cbd-63108e62ec80" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498411}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "40cf6769-0da8-5620-85fa-c881e7916a83" :question/math-academy-id "q-143080" :question/problem "The length, in centimeters, of newborn children in a particular hospital is distributed with a population standard deviation of $\\sigma =5\\,cm.$ On a particular day in a nursery room of the hospital, there are $64$ newborn children, and their mean length is $49.5\\,cm.$ Find a $99%$ confidence interval for the population mean length $\\mu $ of the children born in that hospital.\n\n*Hint: Given a random variable $Z∼N(0,1),$ the table below shows the $z$-scores ${z}_{p}$ such that $P(Z>{z}_{p})=p$ for some particular values of $p.$*\n\n| $p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${z}_{p}$ | $1.282$ | $1.645$ | $1.960$ | $2.326$ | $2.576$ |" :question/worked-solution "We do not know the distribution of the population. However, the sample size $n=64\\ge 30$ is *sufficiently large*. Therefore, according to the central limit theorem, we may use the following approximation:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\overset{―}{X}-\\mu }{\\sigma /\\sqrt{n}}∼N(0,1),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $\\overset{―}{X}$ is the sample mean,\n\n- $\\mu $ is the population mean,\n\n- ${\\sigma }^{2}$ is the population variance, and\n\n- $n$ is the sample size.\n\nGiven a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ of the original distribution is given by\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}\\pm {z}_{\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\frac{\\sigma }{\\sqrt{n}},\n$$\n\nwhere $P(Z>{z}_{\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2}$ and $Z∼N(0,1).$\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}=49.5\\,cm,\\,n=64,\\,\\sigma =5\\,cm.\n$$\n\nWe are interested in finding a $99%$ confidence interval. So, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.99=0.01\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.005.\n$$\n\nWe also need to find the $z$-score value ${z}_{0.005}$ such that $P(Z>{z}_{0.005})=0.005,$ where $Z∼N(0,1).$\n\n![](images/8c/8c5ac93fdb7b03a2bf9cad38c9e5687867a2fa2968120586f3bf2e6101c24cbe.png)\n\nFrom the percentage points table of the normal distribution, we obtain that ${z}_{0.005}=2.576:$ \n\n| $p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${z}_{p}$ | $1.282$ | $1.645$ | $1.960$ | $2.326$ | $2.576$ |\n\nTherefore, the $99%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ is the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}49.5\\pm 2.576\\cdot \\frac{5}{\\sqrt{64}} \\\\ 49.5\\pm 1.61\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
